= Johari window — Technical =

==Definition and provenance==
The Johari window is a descriptive two-axis classification of information about a focal person relative to a specified audience. Its historical source is Joseph Luft and Harrington Ingham's 1955 presentation, ''The Johari Window: A Graphic Model of Interpersonal Awareness'', in the Proceedings of the Western Training Laboratory in Group Development.<ref name="original">Luft, J., and Ingham, H. (1955). ''The Johari Window: A Graphic Model of Interpersonal Awareness''. Proceedings of the Western Training Laboratory in Group Development, UCLA, Los Angeles. Bibliographic record: https://bibbase.org/network/publication/ingham-luft-thejohariwindowagraphicmodelofinterpersonalawareness-1955</ref> Luft later described the window explicitly as a heuristic and recalled its first presentation with Ingham in 1955; the original proceedings citation is a bibliographic record, not a retrieved full-text copy.<ref name="luft">Joseph Luft, [https://lsa.umich.edu/globalscholars/ala-212-course-pack/privilege-and-feedback.html The Johari Window: A Graphic Model of Awareness in Interpersonal Relations], author account reproduced by the University of Michigan.</ref> The American Psychological Association describes the model as a four-pane representation of communication and awareness, rather than a diagnostic instrument.<ref name="apa">American Psychological Association. “Johari window.” ''APA Dictionary of Psychology'', updated April 19, 2018. https://dictionary.apa.org/johari-window</ref>

==Analytical representation==
For a specified proposition ''p'' about a person, define two context-dependent predicates: ''S(p)'' = the person recognizes ''p'', and ''O(p)'' = a specified other person or group recognizes ''p''. The four cells are the combinations:
{| class="wikitable"
! S(p) !! O(p) !! Johari area
|-
| yes || yes || Open
|-
| no || yes || Blind
|-
| yes || no || Hidden
|-
| no || no || Unknown
|}
This binary representation is an '''analytical restatement''', not a measurement scale claimed by the original authors. Real-world awareness is partial, contested and audience-specific. “Known by others” cannot be treated as objective truth: an observer may be confidently wrong. Some propositions are ambiguous or impossible to verify, so not every item can be assigned reliably.

==Mechanisms proposed in practice==
The framework suggests that voluntary self-disclosure can make formerly private information mutually known, while accurate and appropriately delivered feedback can make an unnoticed behavior apparent to its subject. The direction and benefit of any such change depend on consent, trust, information accuracy and the social setting. Neither operation guarantees that the open area grows or that interpersonal outcomes improve. The metaphor of changing quadrant sizes should not be read as a conserved physical quantity.

==Worked example==
Consider a project meeting in which a member notices their own anxiety but has not mentioned it. Relative to colleagues who have not inferred it, that information is ''hidden''. After the member voluntarily explains their concern, it may become ''open''. In another case, peers report repeated interruptions; if the member was unaware and the report is accurate, this may move from ''blind'' to ''open'' after reflection. If the report is biased or inaccurate, there is no warranted move to “shared knowledge.” These examples are illustrative, not empirical observations.

==Evidence and limitations==
The 1955 source establishes the model's provenance; the APA dictionary establishes its recognized descriptive definition. Neither source, by itself, demonstrates causal efficacy of workshops, universal quadrant proportions, predictive validity or a psychometrically validated four-factor structure. Claims that an exercise improves trust, teamwork or performance require independently designed evaluations, defined outcomes and appropriate comparison groups. The model is best understood as a heuristic that makes perspectives discussable. A workshop evaluation should define the audience and information items, obtain consent, distinguish agreement from accuracy using an independent criterion where feasible, and assess pre-specified outcomes against an appropriate comparator with follow-up. Inter-rater agreement is not criterion validity.

===Ethical considerations===
Self-disclosure may expose private information or unequal power relations. Feedback can be affected by stereotypes, interpersonal conflict, or differential access to behavior. Facilitators should distinguish consent from pressure, avoid interpreting non-disclosure as pathology, and allow disagreement about observers' impressions.

==Relation to other approaches==
The Johari window classifies the distribution of perceived knowledge; it does not replace empirical measures of self–other agreement, structured feedback protocols or clinical assessment. Its four quadrants should not be confused with fixed personality traits or biological modules. Vazire's self–other knowledge asymmetry study compared self and informant ratings against behavioral or other criteria and investigated when different perspectives yield more accurate trait judgments.<ref name="soka">S. Vazire, [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20085401/ Who knows what about a person? The self-other knowledge asymmetry (SOKA) model], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 98, 281–300 (2010).</ref> This is related empirical research, not validation of the four Johari quadrants or proof of workshop efficacy. No inference to AI self-awareness follows from these human studies.

